  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cooler air mass will continue to advect into the area this\nmorning on steady NW winds. Gusts have come down from their peak\nlast evening, but may perk up again after sunrise as the\nboundary layer deepens. Gusts to around 25 mph will be possible\nthrough the morning and will subside this afternoon as the\ngradient slackens with high pressure moving closer to overhead.\nIt will be much cooler today with highs ranging from the upper\n30s near the Canadian Border to low 50s across the south. The\ncooler airmass combined with favorable radiational cooling\nconditions will bring lows into the 20s tonight.\n\nTemperatures moderate Sunday into Monday as high pressure\nsettles south of New England. Another area of high pressure over\nSE Canada pushes south Tuesday and may send a backdoor front\nthrough the area that could knock temperatures down a few\ndegrees. However, the NBM still keeps temperatures above normal\nwith highs ranging from the 50s north to 60s south. Ensembles\nsuggest troughing over the western US will slowly migrate east\nthrough the week. Here, across the Northeast conditions likely\nstay dry through at least Wednesday with precipitation chances\nslowly rising late in the week as the trough approaches the East\nCoast.",
